1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,020
A million evaluations to price an option on a hundred stocks: an average over a hundred inputs.

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:09,820
Spread them as a grid; that feels thorough.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,360
How many values does each input get?

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:14,960
One.

5
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,800
The whole grid is one point: the centre.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,650
Two values per input would take two to the hundredth points.

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:30,790
Since Metropolis and Ulam's 1949 paper, the standard tool has been random points, like this one.

8
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:34,160
How far off would a million random points land?

9
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,280
Start with two inputs.

10
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:43,610
Our function is one inside this quarter disc, zero outside: its average is exactly one half.

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:49,700
A ten by ten grid puts fifty-two of its hundred points inside: two points off.

12
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:54,566
A hundred random darts put forty-four inside: six points off.

13
00:00:54,816 --> 00:00:57,096
In two dimensions, the grid wins.

14
00:00:57,533 --> 00:01:00,663
Now give each a million points, and add inputs.

15
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:05,043
The disc becomes a ball, grown to cover exactly half the box.

16
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:13,070
The grid's million points give each axis a thousand values in two dimensions, then a hundred, ten, three.

17
00:01:13,666 --> 00:01:17,446
From twenty dimensions on, just the centre, inside the ball.

18
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:21,236
So the grid says the ball fills the box: fifty points off.

19
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,720
Even two values on nineteen axes is eighteen points off, while the darts stay under a tenth of a point.

20
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:35,100
Maybe darts win by giving every axis more values.

21
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:45,020
A million points on the diagonal give every axis a million values, and still miss by eight points: their coordinates move in lockstep.

22
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:56,376
A dart's coordinates are drawn independently, so it lands inside with a chance equal to the answer, in any dimension: each dart is a coin flip.

23
00:01:57,033 --> 00:01:59,313
One flip is either zero or one.

24
00:01:59,463 --> 00:02:04,083
Its average is a half, so every flip misses by exactly a half.

25
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,190
Repeat the hundred-dart experiment three hundred times.

26
00:02:08,390 --> 00:02:10,780
In both, the estimates spread this wide.

27
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:19,353
Those misses fall up or down at random, so they partly cancel: sixteen darts stray about four halves, not sixteen.

28
00:02:19,753 --> 00:02:22,013
N darts stray about root N halves.

29
00:02:22,213 --> 00:02:26,233
Divide by N, and the typical miss is a half over root N.

30
00:02:27,033 --> 00:02:29,523
For a hundred darts: five points.

31
00:02:30,023 --> 00:02:31,623
We measured four point eight.

32
00:02:31,923 --> 00:02:36,893
Any other function swaps the half for its own spread, sigma; there's still no d.

33
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:40,953
So, back to our ball: a million darts, any dimension.

34
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:43,493
How far off is a typical estimate?

35
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:48,583
One half over a thousand: five hundredths of a point.

36
00:02:50,033 --> 00:02:58,613
In 2012, Bergstra and Bengio found random search tuned neural networks as well as grid search, in a fraction of the time.

37
00:02:59,063 --> 00:03:06,603
If one setting matters most, nine grid trials test three values of it; nine random trials test nine.

38
00:03:07,366 --> 00:03:10,486
Darts do have a blind spot: tiny answers.

39
00:03:10,686 --> 00:03:16,596
A radius-one ball fills two parts in a hundred trillion of a thirty-dimensional box.

40
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:22,876
Below this line, a million darts expect under one hit, so they usually report zero.

41
00:03:24,566 --> 00:03:26,706
The darts never noticed the axes:

42
00:03:27,106 --> 00:03:29,786
a million coin flips, in any dimension.

